                  <h2>How to Detect a Brain Tumor at Home: A Practical Symptom Checklist</h2>
                  <p>While you cannot diagnose a tumor yourself, you can gather information that makes your doctor’s assessment faster and more accurate:</p>
                  <ol>
                    <li><strong>Keep a symptom diary.</strong> Note the date, time, duration and severity of each headache or episode on a scale of 1 to 10, what you were doing, and whether there was vomiting, a change in vision or weakness.</li>
                    <li><strong>Look for patterns.</strong> Are headaches worse in the morning? Do they wake you up? Are they becoming more frequent or needing more painkillers?</li>
                    <li><strong>Ask family members to observe.</strong> Changes in speech, memory, mood or walking are often easier for others to notice.</li>
                    <li><strong>Film an episode if it is safe.</strong> A short phone video of a suspected seizure helps a neurologist enormously.</li>
                    <li><strong>Write down your medical history.</strong> Include any past cancer, since cancers of the lung, breast, kidney and bowel, as well as melanoma, are the most common sources of brain metastases.</li>
                    <li><strong>Do not rely on painkillers.</strong> Taking more and more tablets can mask a problem that is getting worse.</li>
                  </ol>
                  <p>Think of this checklist as preparation for a doctor’s visit, never as a replacement for one.</p>

                  <h2>Red Flags: When to Go to the Emergency Department</h2>
                  <p>No attempt to detect a brain tumor at home should ever delay emergency care. Call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department straight away if you or a family member has:</p>
                  <ul>
                    <li>A sudden, severe, “worst ever” headache</li>
                    <li>A seizure lasting more than five minutes, or repeated seizures</li>
                    <li>Sudden weakness, facial drooping, slurred speech or confusion, which can also be signs of a stroke</li>
                    <li>Sudden loss of vision or double vision</li>
                    <li>A headache with fever, a stiff neck and drowsiness</li>
                    <li>Any loss of consciousness</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p>These symptoms need urgent assessment whether or not a tumor turns out to be the cause.</p>
