                  <h2>How Doctors Actually Diagnose a Brain Tumor</h2>
                  <p>Once you have tried to detect a brain tumor at home by carefully noting your symptoms, a doctor confirms or rules it out through these steps:</p>
                  <ol>
                    <li><strong>Neurological examination:</strong> checks of reflexes, strength, coordination, sensation, hearing, vision and memory.</li>
                    <li><strong>Eye examination:</strong> swelling of the optic disc, called papilledema, can reveal raised pressure inside the skull.</li>
                    <li><strong>MRI with contrast:</strong> the most accurate scan for detecting and mapping brain tumors.</li>
                    <li><strong>CT scan:</strong> faster and widely available, often used in emergencies or when MRI is not possible.</li>
                    <li><strong>Biopsy or surgery:</strong> a tissue sample confirms the exact tumor type and grade, which guides treatment.</li>
                    <li><strong>Whole-body evaluation:</strong> if the tumor looks like a metastasis, a CT or PET-CT scan searches for the original cancer elsewhere.</li>
                  </ol>
