                  <h2>Brain Metastases: Why Cancer Patients Need Extra Vigilance</h2>
                  <p>For people already living with cancer, new neurological symptoms need special attention. Brain metastases can appear months or even years after the original diagnosis, and catching them early widens the treatment options.</p>
                  <p>Patients who have received <a href="/services/lung-cancer-treatment-hyderabad">lung cancer treatment in Hyderabad</a> are advised to report persistent headaches or balance problems early, because some lung cancers have a known tendency to spread to the brain. The same applies after <a href="/services/breast-cancer-treatment-hyderabad">breast cancer surgery and treatment</a>, particularly for HER2-positive and triple-negative types, and during follow-up for <a href="/services/colorectal-cancer-treatment-hyderabad">colon and rectal cancer</a> or <a href="/services/urological-cancer-treatment-hyderabad">kidney and other urological cancers</a>. Tumors of the sinuses, skull base and nasopharynx, managed as part of <a href="/services/head-neck-cancer-treatment-hyderabad">head and neck cancer care</a>, can also cause facial numbness, double vision or headaches by growing close to the brain.</p>
                  <p>Routine blood markers, such as <a href="/blog/esr-level-in-cancer-patients">the ESR level in cancer patients</a>, cannot detect spread to the brain, so symptoms and imaging remain the key. When brain metastases are found early, focused radiation (stereotactic radiosurgery), surgery and modern targeted drugs tend to work better.</p>
