                  <h2>Which Doctor Should You See?</h2>
                  <p>If your efforts to detect a brain tumor at home have turned up worrying symptoms and you have no history of cancer, start with a general physician or neurologist, who can examine you and order an MRI. Confirmed brain tumors are treated by neurosurgeons, neuro-oncologists and radiation oncologists working together. If you are already under cancer care, inform your oncologist straight away.</p>
                  <p>Dr. Anvesh Dharanikota’s practice at Arete Hospitals, Gachibowli, offers <a href="/services">surgical oncology services for cancers across the body</a> and discusses suspected brain involvement with neurosurgery and radiation oncology specialists through multidisciplinary tumor boards. You can also read <a href="/testimonials">patient testimonials about their cancer care experience</a> or browse <a href="/faqs">common questions about cancer surgery and follow-up</a>.</p>

                  <h2>The Final Word</h2>
                  <p>Learning how to detect a brain tumor at home really means learning which symptoms to take seriously. Most headaches are harmless, but a changing headache pattern, a first seizure or steadily progressive neurological symptoms should lead to a prompt medical evaluation and, when advised, an MRI. Early assessment brings either reassurance or a head start on treatment, and both are worth having.</p>
